5117188, 0.0034465789794921875, -0.00820159912109375, 0.03399658203125, 0.040435791015625, -0.03192138671875, -0.0191497802734375, -0.0130767822265625, -0.0172576904296875, 0.0037097930908203125, 0.034942626953125, -0.0037593841552734375, 0.01013946533203125, 0.0163116455078125, -0.0213775634765625, 0.00603485107421875, 0.036224365234375, 0.00652313232421875, 0.03997802734375, -0.006877899169921875, 0.00861358642578125, -0.006420135498046875, 0.041015625, 0.017578125, 0.016845703125, 0.0113677978515625, -0.0184173583984375, -0.0108184814453125, 0.0046539306640625, -0.004436492919921875, -0.0195465087890625, 0.038482666015625, 0.008758544921875, 0.03668212890625, -0.0265960693359375, 0.0102996826171875, -0.0172271728515625, 0.004390716552734375, 0.010589599609375, -0.011444091796875, 0.002117156982421875, -0.0032100677490234375, -0.0292816162109375, 0.0271759033203125, -0.023590087890625, -0.0202178955078125, 0.0106658935546875, -0.0009160041809082031, 0.01410675048828125, 0.006927490234375, 0.036468505859375, 0.04425048828125, -0.007427215576171875, -0.0178070068359375, 0.0011730194091796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance, and to compare its performance to existing manual review practices.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. The multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three sequential agents: (1) a structuring agent that segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using regular expression rules and a local large language model (LLM); (2) a consistency-checking agent that detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections and within sections; and (3) a QA agent that identified gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented communication of critical findings. The workflow proceeded as follows: the structuring agent first assigned each sentence to an anatomical section, producing a structured report; this structured output was then passed to the consistency-checking agent, which analyzed section content for mismatches; finally, the QA agent reviewed the flagged outputs for gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented critical findings. Each agent\u2019s output served as the direct input for the next, and all processing was performed locally to ensure data privacy.\n\nTechnical Details: The structuring agent used regular expressions to identify section headers (e.g., \"Lungs:\", \"Liver:\", \"Kidneys:\") and assign sentences accordingly. Sentences not matching predefined headers were processed by a local LLM based on the Llama-2-7B architecture, fine-tuned on 5,000 de-identified radiology reports from the same institution. The tokenizer used was the default Llama-2 tokenizer. Fine-tuning used an 80/20 training/validation split, a batch size of 16, learning rate of 2e-5, and 3 epochs on an NVIDIA A100 GPU running PyTorch 2.0 and CUDA 11.8. Hyperparameters were selected based on preliminary grid search for optimal validation accuracy. The LLM was validated on a holdout set of 200 reports, achieving 87% accuracy in section assignment. Regular expressions were iteratively refined based on error analysis.\n\nManual Review Baseline: Manual review was conducted by two board-certified radiologists, who independently reviewed the structuring and error flagging for each report. Reviewers were blinded to system outputs. Disagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. The time required for manual review averaged 8 minutes per report. Inter-rater reliability was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa for both structuring and error flagging.\n\nError Flagging Criteria: A \"section mismatch\" was defined as a clinically relevant finding\u2014specifically, any abnormality or diagnosis with potential to alter patient management (e.g., pulmonary nodule, liver lesion, bowel obstruction)\u2014present in the Findings section but absent or contradictory in the Impression section, or vice versa. For example, a pulmonary nodule described in Findings but not mentioned in Impression, or an Impression stating \"no acute findings\" despite abnormal Findings, constituted a mismatch. A \"gender-anatomy conflict\" was flagged when a report referenced anatomy inconsistent with the patient\u2019s recorded gender (e.g., mention of \"prostate\" in a female patient). \"Undocumented critical findings\" were flagged when a critical finding (e.g., pneumothorax, active hemorrhage) was described in Findings but lacked documentation of communication in the Impression or dedicated communication section. Examples of each error type were reviewed by both radiologists, who confirmed operational definitions by consensus.\n\nSampling Strategy: For radiologist evaluation, 45 reports were selected using stratified random sampling to ensure representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) and anatomical regions (chest, abdomen, pelvis). The sample size was determined based on feasibility for expert review and to ensure at least 10 examples of each error type. However, the small sample size limits statistical power, as reflected in wide confidence intervals, and may not be fully representative of the broader dataset. This limitation impacts the generalizability of the findings and should be addressed in future studies with larger samples.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: Evaluation metrics were defined as follows:\n- \"Correctly restructured\": all sentences accurately assigned to the appropriate anatomical section without loss or fabrication of information, as determined by consensus radiologist review.\n- \"Incorrectly restructured\": misassignment of sentences or alteration of clinical content.\n- QA performance ratings: \"excellent\" (no errors, all flagged issues correctly identified, and no unflagged errors), \"good\" (minor errors present, such as a single missed or incorrectly flagged issue not affecting clinical interpretation), and \"fair\" (errors with potential to affect clinical interpretation, such as missed critical findings or major section misassignments).\n- Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa.\n- System-level accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated against the radiologist consensus for each error type.\n\nA summary of key evaluation metrics is provided in Table 1.\n\nTable 1. Summary of System Performance Metrics\n\n| Error Type                    | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1-score |\n|-------------------------------|----------|-----------|--------|----------|\n| Section mismatches            | 89%      | 81%       | 77%    | 79%      |\n| Gender-anatomy conflicts      | 99%      | 100%      | 83%    | 91%      |\n| Undocumented critical findings| 98%      | 80%       | 75%    | 77%      |\n\nResults:\n\nStructuring Agent Performance:\n- The structuring agent processed all 638 reports (22,270 sentences), assigning sentences to predefined anatomical sections while retaining original content.\n- Compared to manual review, the agent achieved 87% accuracy in section assignment on the validation set.\n\nError Types and Flagging:\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%): 80 (12.5%) for section mismatches, 6 (0.9%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 4 (0.6%) for undocumented critical findings.\n- For section mismatches, the most common errors were discrepancies between Findings and Impression sections, such as a pulmonary nodule described in Findings but omitted from Impression.\n- All flagged gender-anatomy conflicts involved references to gender-specific anatomy inconsistent with patient gender.\n- Undocumented critical findings were flagged when critical findings were mentioned in Findings but not documented as communicated.\n- Overall error detection metrics are summarized in Table 1.\n\nRadiologist Agreement and QA Performance:\n- Both reviewers agreed that 31/45 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%).\n- Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56); for error flagging, kappa = 0.61.\n- Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" in 24/45 reports (53%), \"good\" in 14/45 (31%), and \"fair\" in 7/45 (16%).\n\nError Propagation Analysis: Error propagation was assessed by tracking whether misassignments by the structuring agent led to missed or incorrect error flags in downstream agents. In the evaluation subset, 3/45 reports (7%) showed evidence of error propagation, where a misassigned section led to a missed or incorrect error flag in subsequent agents. This was determined by comparing the sequence of agent outputs and identifying cases where an upstream error directly resulted in a downstream error. Ablation analysis (removing the structuring agent and using unstructured reports) reduced overall error detection F1-score by 12%, indicating that structuring accuracy impacts downstream QA performance and system robustness.\n\nLimitations: This study was limited to a single institution and a specific set of CT reports, which may affect generalizability. The AI system\u2019s performance may vary with different report styles or modalities. The evaluation subset was relatively small, limiting statistical power and representativeness, as reflected in wide confidence intervals. Radiologist variability in interpretation and the subjective nature of some QA ratings may introduce bias. Sequential agent operation can propagate errors from one stage to the next, as quantified above. Potential mitigation strategies include further refinement of the structuring agent, implementation of error correction mechanisms, and validation in larger, more diverse datasets. Further benchmarking against other automated approaches is needed before clinical deployment.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system, comprising structuring, consistency-checking, and QA agents, enabled automated radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system demonstrated moderate accuracy in structuring and QA tasks, with most errors correctly identified and minimal risk of information loss or fabrication in the evaluated sample. While these findings suggest potential for improving standardization and quality assurance in radiology practice, further benchmarking and validation in larger, more diverse datasets are necessary before clinical implementation."}}